<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=electroplating">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electroplating</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as electrochemical deposition, electrodeposition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Electroplating is a process that coats an object with metal by passing a direct electric current through a solution of a salt of that metal, so that the metal's cations are reduced onto the object, which serves as the cathode. The Italian chemist Luigi Valentino Brugnatelli carried out the first electrodeposition in 1805, using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">voltaic pile</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-volta">Alessandro Volta</a> had invented five years earlier. The French Academy of Sciences suppressed his work, and it was not used in general industry for the following thirty years. George and Henry Elkington were awarded the first patents in 1840 and founded the industry in Birmingham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/electroplating.jpg" alt="Electroplating" width="330" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Die_Gartenlaube_(1892)_b_030_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1805</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Luigi Valentino Brugnatelli · George Elkington · Henry Elkington</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Britain and Russia (by 1839)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Research from the 1930s theorised that electroplating had been performed in the Parthian Empire with a device like the Baghdad Battery, but this has since been refuted: the items were fire-gilded using mercury.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electrolysis">Electrolysis<span>1800</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=electroplating">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
